s my knight on a dominant central outpost where it cannot be easily attacked by pawns. From d4, the knight controls key squares (b3, c2, e2, f3, b5, c6, e6, f5), puts pressure on White's position, and supports my central pawn structure. This active piece placement is critical in the middlegame and gives me concrete threats while improving my position.\n\nAnswer: N: c6-d4"}
