te into a defensive position where the king must move, and my two bishops will dominate the board. After the king moves, I can continue coordinating my pieces for a strong attack. This is far superior to quiet moves like developing rooks or capturing pawns, as it maintains initiative and exploits White's king safety issues.\n\nAnswer: B: d3-e2"}
